anism of these effects is  still being worked out so Iran has a  huge population of mustard victims who  are still suffering symptoms tens of  thousands of people it's a unique cohort  to try to develop better strategies to  treat mustard and also if there's some  way to develop chemicals compounds that  can be applied during the initial attack  right now that antidote to sulfur  mustard does not exist  all nerve agents attack an enzyme called  acetylcholinesterase it's vital to the  body it breaks down the neurotransmitter  acetylcholine so it is essential for  maintaining levels of the  neurotransmitter in the synapses one of  the most insidious effects and nerve  agents is that after they bind to sotl  choline esterase this bond over time  hardens becomes permanent essentially  and this is called aging so as acetyl  choline builds up in the synapses it  over stimulates the nerves nerve cells  and that has a variety of effects so it  can lead to seizures convulsions when  the nerve agent enters the brain and  starts interacting with the enzyme there  it sends victims into a coma and unless  they're treated really quickly they will  usually stop breathing so soldiers who  are exposed to nerve agents carry three  countermeasures that they can self  inject or inject into a buddy one is  called atropine it binds to the same  enzyme that the nerve agents bind you  there for preventing the nerve agent  from exerting its effects another is an  oxime that if nerve agent is attached to  the enzyme it prise it off so it's like  a little molecular crowbar and the third  is an anticonvulsant one of the major  symptoms of nerve agent exposure is  seizures and this anticonvulsant takes  care of that's it  for a long time people thought that if  you're supposed to nerve agent either  lived or died if you survived the nerve  agent attack you wouldn't on to live a  normal average healthy life it didn't  have long-term effects people assumed  that began to change especially after  